"""Ethics, professionalism, and public health catalog."""
from qfmt import item as I

ITEMS = [
# --- Ethics ---
I("ethics","eth-consent-elective","Ethics & Professionalism","Consent","easy","F",44,
 "the surgical clinic","needs an elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy for biliary colic",
 "asks what the operation involves",
 "Capacious, English-speaking, not sedated, not coerced.",
 "Ultrasound: stones. LFTs normal.",
 "What is required for valid consent?",
 "Voluntary, informed, and given by a person with capacity, covering material risks a reasonable person in her position would want to know (Rogers v Whitaker / Australian material-risk standard)",
 ["A signature on a form without discussion as sufficient","Consent from her husband as mandatory because she is married","Consent after midazolam premedication as ideal","Listing only benefits and no risks"],
 "Australian consent is a process: capacity, voluntariness, and material risks including those the particular patient would attach significance to.",
 ["The form records consent; it does not replace the conversation.","Marriage does not transfer decision-making.","Sedation impairs capacity.","Risks must be discussed."],
 "Ask 'what matters to you?' — a pianist needs to know about nerve injury.","A signed blank consent in pre-op clinic with no discussion.","Consent requires capacity, voluntariness, and material risks.","Australian charter / Rogers v Whitaker"),

I("ethics","eth-capacity-delirium","Ethics & Professionalism","Capacity","moderate","M",81,
 "the ward","needs IV antibiotics for pneumonia, pulling out cannulae, fluctuating attention",
 "son wants 'everything done', patient currently saying 'no needles' then falling asleep",
 "CAM positive, inattentive, not consistently able to repeat risks.",
 "CXR pneumonia, hypoxic. This is time-critical treatment.",
 "What is the most appropriate consent pathway?",
 "He currently lacks capacity; treat under necessity/duty of care and involve the substitute decision-maker, while using the least restrictive option",
 ["Honour the last shouted 'no' as a capacious refusal during delirium","Wait 3 days without antibiotics for a lucid interval in severe pneumonia","A full guardianship hearing before the first dose in life-threatening infection","Force treatment without any attempt to explain or involve family"],
 "Capacity is decision- and time-specific. Delirium often removes it. Emergency treatment can proceed in the patient's best interests with SDM involvement.",
 ["Fluctuating 'no' in delirium is not a capacious refusal.","Delay is harmful.","Guardianship is not required before emergency antibiotics.","Still treat the person with respect."],
 "Reassess capacity when the delirium clears.","Taking a delirious refusal as an advance directive.","Treat incapacitous emergencies in the patient's best interests.","Australian guardianship / consent law"),

I("ethics","eth-confidential-adolescent","Ethics & Professionalism","Confidentiality","moderate","F",16,
 "the GP clinic","Gillick-competent, requests contraception, does not want her parents told",
 "sexually active with a similar-age boyfriend, no coercion, no abuse flags",
 "Mature enough to understand risks and alternatives. STI screen offered.",
 "Pregnancy test negative.",
 "What is the most appropriate confidentiality approach?",
 "Respect her confidentiality and provide contraception if she is competent, while encouraging (not forcing) parental involvement, unless there is a risk that triggers mandatory reporting",
 ["Always telephone parents before any contraception in a competent 16-year-old","Refuse all care until a parent attends","Publish her request in a waiting-room newsletter","Mandatory police report of consensual similar-age activity with no abuse as automatic in all states without checking the law"],
 "Mature minors can consent (Gillick / Australian equivalents). Confidentiality is owed unless risk of serious harm or mandatory reporting (abuse, some notifiable diseases).",
 ["Competence allows confidential care.","Delaying contraception risks pregnancy.","Confidentiality is a duty.","Consensual peer relationships are not automatically a police matter — know local law and abuse flags."],
 "Document why you judged her competent.","A 'courtesy call' to mum that breaks trust.","Respect competent adolescent confidentiality unless risk requires disclosure.","RACGP / Australian consent to treatment of minors"),

I("ethics","eth-notifiable","Ethics & Professionalism","Confidentiality","easy","M",27,
 "the GP clinic","confirmed pulmonary tuberculosis, still coughing",
 "asks you not to tell anyone, including public health",
 "Infectious, sharing a house, works in a childcare centre.",
 "NAAT and culture pending/positive as per the stem of confirmed TB.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Notify the public-health unit as required by law; confidentiality is not absolute for notifiable infectious diseases",
 ["Honour absolute confidentiality and let him continue at childcare","Tell the local newspaper","No treatment because he refused notification","Notify only after he has left the country"],
 "Notifiable diseases (TB, measles, many STIs as specified) must be reported. Explain why. Work restrictions may apply.",
 ["Childcare exposure is a public-health emergency.","Media is not the pathway.","He still needs treatment.","Delay endangers others."],
 "You can disclose the minimum necessary to the proper authority.","A gentleman's agreement to keep infectious TB secret.","Notify legally notifiable infections.","State public-health Acts / CDNA"),

I("ethics","eth-mandatory-child","Ethics & Professionalism","Mandatory reporting","moderate","M",3,
 "the emergency department","toddler, patterned burns on the buttocks, delayed presentation, changing history",
 "parents want to leave before a full assessment",
 "Burns spare the flexures in a dip pattern, no splash, inconsistent with 'pulled a cup of tea'.",
 "Skeletal survey and child-protection pathway indicated.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Do not let the child leave; admit, treat, and make a mandatory child-protection report",
 ["Allow discharge because the parents are 'nice'","Wait for a confession before reporting","Photograph for social media teaching without consent/governance","Confront the parent in the waiting room as the first forensic step"],
 "Doctors are mandatory reporters. Patterned injury plus inconsistent history is child abuse until proven otherwise. Safety first.",
 ["Niceness is not a risk tool.","Suspicion, not proof, triggers a report.","Teaching photos have strict rules.","Forensic interviews are specialist."],
 "The child is the patient.","A discharge against advice in a 3-year-old with patterned burns.","Report suspected child abuse and keep the child safe.","State mandatory-reporting laws / RCH"),

I("ethics","eth-elder-abuse","Ethics & Professionalism","Mandatory reporting","moderate","F",84,
 "the GP clinic","niece is carer, new bruises, unpaid bills, patient looks frightened when niece speaks",
 "weight loss, missed medicines",
 "Bruises of different ages, poor hygiene, niece answers all questions.",
 "See the patient alone. Bank statements not your first tool — safety is.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "See her alone, assess capacity and safety, and refer to elder-abuse/aged-care and, if required, police or public guardian pathways",
 ["Ignore because elder abuse is rare","Demand the niece stay in the room for 'support'","Immediate guardianship without assessment because she is 84","A public Facebook post to shame the niece"],
 "Elder abuse is common and under-recognised. Financial, physical, and neglect forms exist. Mandatory reporting in residential aged care has specific rules; in the community, duty of care still applies.",
 ["It is not rare.","The carer may be the perpetrator.","Age ≠ incapacity.","Public shaming is unprofessional and unsafe."],
 "Ask the older person alone.","Writing 'family matter' on patterned bruises.","Assess and refer suspected elder abuse.","ACSQHC / elder-abuse guidance"),

I("ethics","eth-boundaries","Ethics & Professionalism","Professional boundaries","easy","F",35,
 "the GP clinic","you are her GP; she asks you to dinner as a date and to be Facebook friends",
 "no emergency, she is a current patient",
 "Well. The request is social.",
 "No dual relationship yet.",
 "What is the most appropriate response?",
 "Decline a romantic/social dual relationship with a current patient; explain the professional boundary and offer to transfer care if needed",
 ["Accept dinner but not Facebook","Start a sexual relationship if she consents because consent cures the power imbalance","Post her health details on your personal social media","Meet after hours in your home for 'counselling'"],
 "AHPRA/Medical Board: sexual and close personal relationships with current patients are prohibited. Power imbalance is the issue, not 'consent'.",
 ["Either social channel is a dual relationship.","Consent does not cancel the ethical prohibition.","Confidentiality still applies.","Home visits for dating are not care."],
 "If you would not write it in the notes, don't do it.","'It was after hours so it didn't count'.","Do not date current patients.","Medical Board of Australia / Good medical practice"),

I("ethics","eth-gifts","Ethics & Professionalism","Professional boundaries","easy","M",70,
 "the GP clinic","after you diagnosed his melanoma early, he offers $5000 cash 'as a thank you'",
 "he is still your patient",
 "No coercion from you. He is capacious.",
 "Practice has a gifts policy.",
 "What is the most appropriate approach?",
 "Decline a large cash gift that could influence care; a small token may be acceptable per policy, with documentation",
 ["Accept $5000 into your personal account without telling anyone","Demand more money","Accept and reduce his fees secretly in a way that looks like a kickback","Ridicule him in the waiting room"],
 "Small tokens of thanks can be acceptable; large gifts create obligation and must be declined or handled via a governance pathway (e.g. donation to the hospital foundation).",
 ["Personal large cash gifts are not OK.","Do not solicit.","Kickbacks are unlawful.","Respect his gratitude while declining."],
 "If it would look bad on the front page, don't take it.","Cash in an envelope in the desk drawer.","Decline gifts that could influence care.","Medical Board of Australia"),

I("ethics","eth-impaired-colleague","Ethics & Professionalism","Professionalism","moderate","M",45,
 "the hospital","you smell alcohol on a consultant colleague about to start a list, slurred speech",
 "you are the registrar",
 "Patients are in the anaesthetic bay. He denies any problem and tells you to mind your own business.",
 "No time for a long debate.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Do not let him operate; escalate immediately to the clinical director/AHA pathway so patients are protected, and support a health response for the colleague",
 ["Ignore it because he is senior","Tweet about it","Join him for a drink then start the list","Wait until after a complication to report"],
 "Patient safety comes first. Impaired colleagues must be stood down from safety-critical work. There is a duty to notify the hospital and, if required, AHPRA.",
 ["Seniority does not override safety.","Public shaming is not the pathway.","You would be complicit.","Prevention beats a coroner."],
 "You are protecting patients and, ultimately, the colleague.","A quiet word and then letting the list proceed.","Stop an impaired colleague from practising now.","Medical Board / AHPRA mandatory notifications"),

I("ethics","eth-error-disclosure","Ethics & Professionalism","Medical errors","moderate","F",62,
 "the ward","you prescribed the wrong insulin dose; she had documented hypoglycaemia that was treated; she is now stable",
 "family have not been told",
 "She is capacious and asking why she felt so unwell.",
 "Incident report not yet filed.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Open disclosure: apologise, explain what happened, what it means, and what will be done to prevent recurrence, and report the incident",
 ["Hide the error because she recovered","Blame the nurse in front of the family as the whole response","Alter the notes to the intended dose","Refuse to document the hypoglycaemia"],
 "Australian Open Disclosure: patients have a right to be told about harm. Apology is not an automatic admission of liability in the way people fear, and it is the ethical (and policy) standard.",
 ["Recovery does not cancel the duty.","Systems and personal accountability, not scapegoating.","Altering notes is serious professional misconduct.","The record must be honest."],
 "Say sorry early.","A cover-up that then comes out in court.","Disclose harmful errors openly.","ACSQHC Australian Open Disclosure Framework"),

I("ethics","eth-futile-eol","Ethics & Professionalism","End-of-life care","moderate","M",88,
 "the ICU","end-stage dementia, bedbound, third aspiration pneumonia, family split, no ACD",
 "one son demands CPR, intubation, and dialysis 'because mum would have wanted everything'",
 "The patient cannot participate. Prognosis is poor. Substitute decision-maker is unclear.",
 "This is not a reversible single-organ problem in an otherwise robust person.",
 "What is the most appropriate ethical frame?",
 "Treat the patient, not the family conflict: offer appropriate (usually palliative) care, avoid harmful futile interventions, and use ethics/guardianship if the SDM is not acting in the patient's interests",
 ["CPR as mandatory because a relative demands it","Stopping all symptom control because prognosis is poor","A secret slow code as a compromise","Ignoring the son without any meeting"],
 "There is no duty to provide futile treatment. There is a duty to provide comfort. Families are consulted, not given a menu of physiologically impossible options as if they were consumer choices.",
 ["CPR in end-stage dementia is not a right that overrides professional judgement.","Palliation is always indicated.","Slow codes are dishonest.","A structured family meeting is required."],
 "Offer what will help; do not offer what will only harm.","A 20-minute resuscitation in a cachectic end-stage patient because nobody had the conversation.","Do not provide futile treatment; do provide palliation.","ACSQHC end-of-life / state guardianship"),

I("ethics","eth-sdm","Ethics & Professionalism","Substitute decision-making","easy","F",76,
 "the ward","incapacitous after a stroke, needs a feeding decision",
 "two children disagree",
 "She appointed her daughter as medical treatment decision-maker on a valid form last year. The son is louder.",
 "Speech pathology: unsafe swallow. ACD is silent on feeding.",
 "Who should decide?",
 "The appointed substitute decision-maker (her daughter), using substituted judgement of the patient's values, not the louder relative",
 ["The louder son automatically","A hospital Twitter poll","The intern alone without family if an SDM exists","Whoever arrives first to the ward"],
 "A valid appointed SDM outranks informal relatives. The SDM must decide as the patient would have, not as the SDM prefers.",
 ["Volume is not legal authority.","Public polls are unprofessional.","Use the SDM.","Arrival time is irrelevant."],
 "Ask for the paperwork early.","A family-meeting shouting match without checking who is the SDM.","The appointed SDM decides, using the patient's values.","State medical-treatment decision-maker laws"),

I("ethics","eth-nfr","Ethics & Professionalism","End-of-life care","easy","M",82,
 "the ward","metastatic pancreatic cancer, capacious, wants no CPR",
 "family want 'full code'",
 "He understands CPR outcomes. Decision is consistent over days.",
 "Documented NFR/Goals-of-care form not yet signed.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Respect his capacious refusal of CPR, complete the goals-of-care form, and explain the decision to the family without letting them override him",
 ["Family veto of a capacious NFR","A slow code to keep the family happy","No documentation because it is 'obvious'","Transfer to ICU against his wishes to avoid the conversation"],
 "A capacious patient can refuse CPR. Families cannot insist on treatment the patient has refused. Document clearly so the night team knows.",
 ["He decides.","Slow codes are unethical.","If it is not written, it may not happen.","ICU is not a conflict-avoidance tool."],
 "Fill in the form before 5 pm Friday.","A full arrest call at 3 am because the form was blank.","Document and respect a capacious NFR.","ACSQHC end-of-life care"),

I("ethics","eth-jw-transfusion","Ethics & Professionalism","Consent","difficult","F",34,
 "the emergency department","Jehovah's Witness, capacious, signed a refusal of blood, now Hb 48 after PPH, still refusing red cells",
 "husband agrees with her, she is oriented and consistent",
 "HR 120, BP 90/60, still refusing blood after a capacity assessment.",
 "Cell salvage/iron/EPO/tranexamic acid as adjuncts. She has an advance refusal of transfusion.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Respect a capacious refusal of blood; use all non-blood strategies and document meticulously",
 ["Transfuse against her will because you disagree with her religion","Wait until she loses consciousness and then transfuse despite a valid refusal","Ridicule her beliefs in the notes","Refuse all other care because she refused blood"],
 "A capacious adult may refuse life-saving blood. Treat with respect and every acceptable alternative. Children are a different legal pathway.",
 ["Your values do not override hers.","A valid advance refusal survives unconsciousness.","Notes must be respectful.","You still provide all other care."],
 "Involve haematology and the hospital liaison committee early.","A covert transfusion 'for her own good'.","Respect capacious refusal of transfusion.","Australian consent law / ANZSBT-aligned"),

I("ethics","eth-aboriginal-atsi","Ethics & Professionalism","Cultural considerations","moderate","M",58,
 "the oncology clinic","Aboriginal man, lung cancer, family want a yarning circle before a decision, he does not want to be the first to hear 'the number'",
 "mistrust of hospitals after a relative's death",
 "He is capacious. An Aboriginal liaison officer is available.",
 "Staging complete. Treatment options exist.",
 "What is the most appropriate approach?",
 "Use culturally safe care: involve Aboriginal health workers, allow family decision processes, and avoid forcing a Western individual-disclosure script if he prefers family-first communication — while still ensuring he can access information",
 ["Refuse a liaison officer because 'we treat everyone the same'","Force a prognosis number in the first sentence against his request","Exclude family as a default","Assume he does not want active treatment because of ethnicity"],
 "Cultural safety is a clinical skill. Many Aboriginal families use collective decision-making. 'Treating everyone the same' is not equity.",
 ["Sameness is not fairness.","Warning shots and preferred communication styles are part of consent.","Family is often central.","Do not ration hope by stereotype."],
 "Ask who should be in the room and how news should be given.","A rushed bad-news dump without a liaison officer.","Practise culturally safe communication.","AHPRA cultural safety / NACCHO"),

I("ethics","eth-interpreter","Ethics & Professionalism","Consent","easy","F",61,
 "the clinic","speaks Mandarin, limited English, needs consent for a procedure",
 "adult son offers to interpret and to 'decide for mum'",
 "She appears to have capacity. The decision is not a trivial blood test — it is a procedure with material risks.",
 "A professional interpreter is available by phone in 5 minutes.",
 "What is the most appropriate communication method?",
 "Use a professional interpreter; do not rely on family for consent to a procedure with material risks",
 ["The son as interpreter and decision-maker by default","Google Translate as equivalent to a professional medical interpreter for consent","No consent needed if English is limited","Shouting in English more slowly as sufficient"],
 "Family interpreters omit, protect, and decide. Professional interpreters are the standard for consent, mental-health, and discharge advice.",
 ["Conflict of interest and accuracy.","Apps miss nuance and confidentiality.","Limited English does not waive consent.","Volume is not language."],
 "Document the interpreter's number.","A 12-year-old child interpreting a cancer diagnosis.","Use professional interpreters for consent.","ACSQHC communication / Australian Charter"),

I("ethics","eth-coi","Ethics & Professionalism","Conflict of interest","easy","M",50,
 "the clinic","you own shares in a company that sells a vitamin drip you are about to recommend",
 "the evidence for the drip is weak",
 "The patient trusts you.",
 "No disclosure has been made.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Do not recommend a weakly evidenced product that profits you; disclose and recuse from the conflict",
 ["Recommend it without disclosure because the shares are 'not that many'","Hide the shareholding in a spouse's name as a solution","Insist they buy it today to hit a sales target","A small kickback from the company as acceptable if the patient 'might benefit'"],
 "Conflicts of interest must be avoided or disclosed and managed. Kickbacks are unlawful. Weak evidence plus personal profit is a hard no.",
 ["Size does not cancel the conflict.","Hiding it is worse.","Sales targets are not clinical indications.","Kickbacks are prohibited."],
 "If you wouldn't want it on the ABC, don't sell it.","A vitamin-drip business on the side of a GP clinic without disclosure.","Avoid and disclose conflicts of interest.","Medical Board of Australia"),

I("ethics","eth-duty-of-care","Ethics & Professionalism","Duty of care","moderate","M",22,
 "the emergency department","intoxicated, head-injured, wants to leave, GCS 13, no responsible adult",
 "abusive when you ask him to stay",
 "CT not yet done. He cannot repeat the risks of leaving.",
 "Security is available. He is not currently capacious.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Detain him for assessment under duty of care / necessary treatment until capacity is regained or a safe plan exists; intoxication plus head injury is not a capacious self-discharge",
 ["Let him wander into traffic because 'liberty'","A police cell as the only medical observation","No documentation of the attempt to keep him","Physical restraint without any attempt at de-escalation or medical indication"],
 "Duty of care can justify detaining an incapacitous, at-risk patient for assessment. This is not a Mental Health Act issue unless there is mental illness.",
 ["Liberty requires capacity.","He needs a medical observation area.","Write everything.","Restraint is last resort."],
 "Intoxication + head injury + wanting to leave = stay.","A signature on a DAMA form from a GCS 13 patient as protection.","Keep incapacitous at-risk patients for assessment.","Australian emergency / consent principles"),

I("ethics","eth-gillick","Ethics & Professionalism","Consent","moderate","F",15,
 "the GP clinic","requests the contraceptive implant, understands mechanism, side-effects, and alternatives, not abused, not coerced",
 "parents overseas for 3 months",
 "Fraser/Gillick criteria met on your assessment. STI screen offered.",
 "Pregnancy test negative.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Provide the implant if she is competent and it is in her best interests; encourage parental involvement but do not make it a barrier",
 ["Refuse because 15 is always incompetent in Australia","Demand a parent on the phone before any discussion of pregnancy risk","A referral to child protection solely because she is sexually active at 15 with a similar-age partner and no abuse flags","The implant as illegal under 18"],
 "Gillick competence is assessed, not assumed from age alone. Contraception can be provided to competent minors. Abuse and age-of-consent laws still need thought.",
 ["15-year-olds can be competent.","A phone demand can delay care.","Sexual activity is not automatically abuse — assess.","Implants are used in this age group when appropriate."],
 "Best interests include preventing pregnancy she does not want.","A blanket 'under 16 no contraception' policy.","Assess Gillick competence for adolescent contraception.","RACGP / Australian paediatric ethics"),

I("ethics","eth-refusal-capacity","Ethics & Professionalism","Consent","moderate","M",55,
 "the ward"," capacious, Jehovah's Witness, refuses blood; separately, a different patient with schizophrenia refuses antibiotics for a gangrenous foot while thought-disordered and believing the foot is 'already saved by aliens'",
 "you are asked which refusal to honour",
 "The first patient can repeat risks. The second cannot weigh information and is at risk of death from a treatable source.",
 "Both say the words 'I refuse'.",
 "What is the distinguishing principle?",
 "Honour capacious refusals; treat incapacitous patients in their best interests (using MHA/guardianship as applicable) — the utterance 'I refuse' is not enough",
 ["All refusals are equally valid regardless of capacity","No patient may ever refuse","Psychosis always removes capacity for every decision including a sandwich","Religion always removes capacity"],
 "Capacity is the gate. A capacious religious refusal is valid. A psychotic, incapacitous refusal of life-saving treatment is not the same act.",
 ["Capacity distinguishes them.","Capacious adults may refuse.","Capacity is decision-specific — he may still choose lunch.","Religion does not equal incapacity."],
 "Assess capacity before you celebrate or override autonomy.","A tick-box 'refused' without a capacity assessment.","Capacity decides whether a refusal is binding.","Australian consent law"),

I("ethics","eth-privacy-celebrity","Ethics & Professionalism","Confidentiality","easy","F",40,
 "the ward","a well-known athlete is admitted under your team",
 "a journalist friend asks you to confirm the diagnosis 'off the record'",
 "You have access to the file because you are on the treating team.",
 "No public statement has been authorised by the patient.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Refuse: confidentiality is owed to every patient, including celebrities; do not confirm or deny",
 ["Confirm off the record because journalists have a public-interest right to all medical facts","Look up a non-team celebrity's file out of curiosity","Post a selfie with the patient","Tell only the diagnosis but not the name"],
 "Curiosity access is an AHPRA matter. Off-the-record is still a breach. Public interest is a high bar (not sports gossip).",
 ["Off the record is still disclosure.","Access must be for care.","Photos need consent and are usually inappropriate.","Diagnosis plus context can identify."],
 "If you are not in the treating team, do not open the file.","A celebrity lookup 'just to see'.","Keep celebrity health information confidential.","AHPRA / Privacy Act"),

I("ethics","eth-social-media","Ethics & Professionalism","Professionalism","easy","M",28,
 "home, after a shift","you want to tweet about a 'hilarious' ED case with enough detail that the town will know who it was",
 "no names, but a rare job plus the date",
 "The patient did not consent to any public discussion.",
 "The tweet would be funny to colleagues.",
 "What is the most appropriate action?",
 "Do not post: de-identified is not unidentifiable in a small community, and professionalism extends to social media",
 ["Post because there is no name","Post the radiograph with the hospital sticker visible","A closed Facebook 'doctors only' group as always safe for identifiable data","Ask a junior to post it instead"],
 "AHPRA social-media guidance: if the patient could recognise themselves, or others could, it is a breach. Closed groups leak.",
 ["No name ≠ anonymous.","Stickers identify.","Closed groups are not a legal safe harbour.","Delegation does not clean the act."],
 "If you have to ask, don't post.","A case tweet that trends in a town of 800.","Do not discuss identifiable cases on social media.","Medical Board social-media guidance"),

I("ethics","eth-research-consent","Ethics & Professionalism","Consent","moderate","F",70,
 "the clinic","a drug company asks you to enrol her in a trial of a new tablet, with a finder's fee per patient",
 "she trusts you completely",
 "The trial has HREC approval. The fee is paid to you personally per recruit.",
 "She would probably say yes if you asked.",
 "What is the most appropriate professional approach?",
 "Disclose the fee and the dual role, separate clinical care from recruitment pressure, and only enrol if it is consistent with her interests and true consent",
 ["Enrol her without mentioning the fee or the research nature","Hide the finder's fee","Threaten to stop being her GP if she refuses","Forge her signature because 'it's only research'"],
 "Research consent is extra, not less, than clinical consent. Personal finder's fees are a conflict and must be disclosed or, better, paid to the institution.",
 ["Research must be explicit.","Hiding money is a conflict.","Undue influence.","Forgery is criminal."],
 "Your patient is not a recruitment quota.","A personal cheque per signature.","Disclose conflicts and obtain real research consent.","NHMRC National Statement"),

I("ethics","eth-conscientious","Ethics & Professionalism","Professionalism","moderate","F",29,
 "the GP clinic","requests a termination referral; your personal beliefs oppose abortion",
 "she is 7 weeks, decided, not coerced",
 "You do not provide the service yourself.",
 "Local public and private pathways exist.",
 "What is the most appropriate professional obligation?",
 "Do not obstruct: inform her of your limitation and provide a timely, effective referral or information so she can access lawful care",
 ["Refuse to see her and withhold all referral information","Shame her as the consultation","Delay until it is too late as a strategy","Report her to her employer"],
 "Conscientious objection is recognised but must not impede access. Australian codes require information and timely referral, not obstruction.",
 ["Obstruction is not a protected conscientious act.","Shame is unprofessional.","Delay is obstruction.","Confidentiality still applies."],
 "Your beliefs are yours; her legal options are hers.","A lecture instead of a referral.","Conscientious objection must not block access to lawful care.","Medical Board / state abortion law"),

I("ethics","eth-chaperone","Ethics & Professionalism","Professionalism","easy","F",24,
 "the GP clinic","needs an intimate examination for a possible Bartholin cyst",
 "anxious, first visit",
 "You are a male GP. A chaperone is available.",
 "The examination is clinically indicated.",
 "What is the most appropriate practice?",
 "Explain, obtain consent, offer a chaperone, and allow her to decline the exam; document the offer",
 ["Examine without explanation because it is 'just a cyst'","Refuse a chaperone because it 'implies you are untrustworthy'","Insist on a student audience without asking","Proceed despite her withdrawing consent mid-exam"],
 "Intimate examinations: explanation, consent, privacy, and a chaperone offer. She can stop the exam at any time.",
 ["Explanation is mandatory.","Chaperones protect both parties.","Students need consent.","Consent can be withdrawn."],
 "Offer a chaperone; don't wait to be asked.","A closed door, no chaperone, no explanation.","Offer a chaperone for intimate examinations.","Medical Board of Australia"),

I("ethics","eth-overtreatment","Ethics & Professionalism","End-of-life care","difficult","M",91,
 "the nursing home","severe frailty, eats little, family want a PEG 'so he doesn't starve', no ACD",
 "recurrent aspiration, bedbound, does not recognise family some days",
 "Speech pathology: unsafe for all consistencies. He previously told a GP he never wanted 'tubes'.",
 "PEG in advanced dementia does not improve survival or aspiration risk.",
 "What is the most appropriate recommendation?",
 "Comfort feeding and palliation; do not recommend a PEG as a solution to advanced dementia aspiration, and explain this to the family",
 ["PEG as routine in all advanced dementia","IV antibiotics forever without a comfort goal","Nil by mouth and no mouth care","A surgical consult as the only ethical option"],
 "PEG feeding in advanced dementia does not prevent aspiration or prolong meaningful life. Good mouth care and careful hand feeding for comfort are the ethical standard unless a reversible problem exists.",
 ["Evidence is against routine PEG.","Antibiotics may still be used case-by-case, not as a default forever.","Mouth care is essential.","Surgery is not required."],
 "Starvation language is powerful — reframe as the disease taking hunger away.","A PEG to ease the family's guilt rather than the patient's suffering.","Do not use PEG as default in advanced dementia.","ANZSPM / geriatric guidance"),

# --- Public health ---
I("public_health","ph-sens-spec","Public Health","Biostatistics","easy","F",50,
 "the journal club","a new blood test for a disease",
 "asks what sensitivity means",
 "The 2×2 table is on the slide.",
 "No clinical patient — this is a methods item framed in a teaching clinic.",
 "What is sensitivity?",
 "The proportion of people with the disease who test positive (true-positive rate)",
 ["The proportion of positive tests that are true disease (that is PPV)","The proportion of people without disease who test negative (that is specificity)","1 − specificity","Prevalence"],
 "Sensitivity = TP/(TP+FN). A sensitive test, when negative, helps rule out (SnNOut).",
 ["That is positive predictive value.","That is specificity.","1 − specificity is the false-positive rate.","Prevalence is the pre-test probability in the population."],
 "SnNOut and SpPIn.","Confusing sensitivity with PPV.","Sensitivity is the true-positive rate among the diseased.","Standard epidemiology"),

I("public_health","ph-ppv-prevalence","Public Health","Biostatistics","moderate","M",45,
 "the GP clinic","a screening test with 99% sensitivity and 99% specificity used in a very low-prevalence setting",
 "a positive result in an asymptomatic man",
 "He wants to know if he 'definitely has it'.",
 "Prevalence in this population is 0.1%.",
 "What happens to positive predictive value?",
 "PPV falls when prevalence is low — most positives may be false positives even with a '99%' test",
 ["PPV is independent of prevalence","PPV equals sensitivity","A 99% specific test never has false positives","NPV falls as prevalence falls"],
 "PPV depends on prevalence. Rare diseases plus imperfect specificity = false-positive burden. This is why we do not PSA-screen without a conversation, and why we Bayesian-update.",
 ["Prevalence drives PPV.","Sensitivity ≠ PPV.","1% of 10,000 healthy people is 100 false positives.","NPV actually rises as prevalence falls."],
 "Ask 'how common is this here?' before trusting a positive screen.","Quoting sensitivity to a patient as if it were PPV.","PPV drops when the disease is rare.","Standard epidemiology / Wilson and Jungner"),

I("public_health","ph-lead-time","Public Health","Screening principles","moderate","F",55,
 "the journal club","a cancer screening program appears to increase survival from diagnosis",
 "no change in age-specific mortality in the population",
 "Lead-time and overdiagnosis are being discussed.",
 "Mortality is the less biased outcome for screening benefit.",
 "What bias is most likely?",
 "Lead-time bias: diagnosis is earlier so survival-from-diagnosis looks longer without changing the date of death",
 ["Recall bias as the best fit for a mortality-unchanged screening program","Confounding by indication in a randomised screening trial with no mortality benefit","Hawthorne effect as the usual explanation of cancer-survival statistics","Berksonian bias in a community screening program"],
 "Lead-time bias is the classic screening trap. Disease-specific mortality in randomised trials is the better endpoint.",
 ["Recall is about memory of exposure.","The stem is screening survival statistics.","Hawthorne is behaviour under observation.","Berkson is hospital-admission bias."],
 "If mortality doesn't move, 'longer survival' may be a calendar trick.","Advertising a screening test with survival-from-diagnosis only.","Lead-time bias inflates survival from diagnosis.","Standard screening theory"),

I("public_health","ph-length-time","Public Health","Screening principles","difficult","M",60,
 "the journal club","screen-detected cancers have better 5-year survival than symptom-detected cancers of the same organ",
 "indolent tumours are more likely to be found by periodic screening",
 "Aggressive interval cancers present between screens.",
 "Overdiagnosis is related.",
 "What bias is illustrated?",
 "Length-time bias: screening preferentially detects slow-growing, more favourable tumours",
 ["Lead-time bias only, with no role for tumour biology","Observer-expectancy as the only bias","Allocation concealment","Placebo effect on tumour doubling time"],
 "Length-time: slow tumours spend longer in the detectable preclinical phase. Screening looks good because it finds the nice cancers.",
 ["Lead-time is earlier diagnosis of the same tumour; length-time is a different mix of tumours.","Not observer bias.","Not an RCT procedure.","Placebo does not slow cancer."],
 "Interval cancers are the nasty ones screening missed.","Claiming screening 'cures more' because screen-detected tumours are biologically milder.","Length-time bias favours detection of indolent tumours.","Standard screening theory"),

I("public_health","ph-nnt","Public Health","Biostatistics","easy","F",62,
 "the clinic","a statin trial: 5-year event rate 4% on placebo, 3% on statin",
 "she asks how many people like her need treatment to prevent one event",
 "ARR = 1% over 5 years.",
 "RRR would be 25%.",
 "What is the 5-year NNT?",
 "100",
 ["4","25","3","1"],
 "NNT = 1/ARR. ARR = 0.04 − 0.03 = 0.01, NNT = 100. RRR (25%) looks more impressive than ARR (1%).",
 ["4 is the placebo event rate as a percent, not NNT.","25 is related to RRR, not NNT.","3 is the treatment event rate.","1 would require an ARR of 100%."],
 "Always ask for absolute risk, not just relative.","A '25% risk reduction' sticker without the 1% absolute.","NNT is 1 divided by the absolute risk reduction.","Standard EBM"),

I("public_health","ph-rr-vs-ar","Public Health","Biostatistics","easy","M",58,
 "the waiting-room poster","'halves your risk' of a rare event from 2 in 1000 to 1 in 1000",
 "he wants to know if this is a huge benefit",
 "You calculate ARR = 0.1%.",
 "RR = 0.5, RRR = 50%.",
 "What is the honest description?",
 "Relative risk is halved but the absolute benefit is 1 in 1000; present both",
 ["Absolute risk is halved from 2% to 1% as stated in the stem (it was 2 in 1000)","NNT is 2","Relative risk reduction is 0.1%","The event is now impossible"],
 "Relative and absolute measures answer different questions. Marketing uses relative risk; counselling should use absolute risk and NNT (here 1000).",
 ["2 in 1000 is 0.2%, not 2%.","NNT = 1/0.001 = 1000.","0.1% is the ARR, not the RRR.","The event still occurs."],
 "Halving a tiny risk is still tiny.","A headline that quotes only RRR.","Quote absolute and relative risk together.","Standard EBM"),

I("public_health","ph-wilson-jungner","Public Health","Screening principles","moderate","F",40,
 "the policy meeting","a proposal to screen the whole adult population with a whole-body MRI",
 "no agreed treatment pathway for incidentalomas, test is expensive, false positives high",
 "You are asked whether this meets screening criteria.",
 "Wilson and Jungner: important disease, acceptable test, known natural history, accepted treatment, cost-effective, continuous process.",
 "What is the main problem?",
 "It fails classic screening criteria: unclear benefit, high false positives, cost, and no agreed treatment pathway for incidental findings",
 ["It meets all Wilson–Jungner criteria easily","Screening never requires a treatment pathway","False positives are irrelevant in screening","Cost is never considered in Australian screening policy"],
 "Good screening programs (NCSP, BreastScreen, NBCSP) exist because they meet criteria. Whole-body MRI as population screening does not.",
 ["It fails several criteria.","Treatment pathway is essential.","False positives cause harm.","Cost-effectiveness is required."],
 "A test can be accurate and still be a bad screening test.","Selling whole-body MRI to the worried well as 'screening'.","Screening must meet Wilson–Jungner-type criteria.","WHO / Australian screening policy"),

I("public_health","ph-outbreak","Public Health","Epidemiology","moderate","M",22,
 "the public-health unit","a cluster of meningococcal disease in a university residential college",
 "you are the on-call PH physician",
 "Two confirmed cases, same serogroup, 4 days apart.",
 "Chemoprophylaxis and vaccination policy exist for close contacts.",
 "What is the most appropriate first public-health action after confirming the diagnoses?",
 "Identify close contacts, offer chemoprophylaxis (and vaccine as indicated), and communicate with the college without causing panic",
 ["Close the entire city","Refuse to notify because of confidentiality","Antibiotics for the whole state empirically","No action until 20 deaths"],
 "Outbreak control: define, count, find contacts, intervene, communicate. Meningococcus has a specific close-contact definition (household, intimate, selected health-care).",
 ["Proportionality.","Notification is required.","Chemoprophylaxis is for close contacts, not a state.","Early action prevents deaths."],
 "Close contacts, not Facebook friends.","Rifampicin for the whole lecture theatre of 400.","Control meningococcal clusters with contact prophylaxis.","CDNA / state PH units"),

I("public_health","ph-needle-stick","Public Health","Prevention","moderate","F",26,
 "the emergency department","nurse, hollow-bore needle-stick from a source patient with known hepatitis C, unknown HIV, HBV-immune nurse",
 "just happened, bleeding the site",
 "Wound washed. Source bloods being obtained with consent/protocol.",
 "Nurse's baseline serology to be drawn.",
 "What is the most appropriate immediate management?",
 "Wash the wound, report, baseline serology, assess HIV PEP window (hours), HBV not needed if immune, HCV follow-up (no routine PEP), and counselling",
 ["No reporting because the source 'looks well'","HIV PEP starting next week as equally effective","Immunoglobulin for HCV as standard PEP","Ignoring HBV status"],
 "Occupational exposure: wash, report, source testing, HIV PEP if indicated (urgently), HBV boost/HBIG if non-immune, HCV is follow-up (DAA if transmission, which is uncommon).",
 ["Looks well is not a risk assessment.","HIV PEP is time-critical.","There is no standard HCV immunoglobulin PEP.","HBV immunity changes the plan."],
 "The first hour matters for HIV PEP.","Squeezing the wound until it is a laceration.","Manage occupational exposure with protocol and timely HIV PEP.","Australian immunisation / state PEP guidelines"),

I("public_health","ph-herd","Public Health","Vaccination","easy","M",1,
 "the GP clinic","parents ask why measles vaccine matters if 'everyone else is vaccinated'",
 "well child, NIP due",
 "No contraindication.",
 "Community coverage has fallen below 95% in their postcode.",
 "What is the public-health principle?",
 "Herd immunity protects those who cannot be vaccinated; measles needs very high coverage because it is highly contagious",
 ["Herd immunity means unvaccinated people never get measles if coverage is 70%","Measles R0 is so low that 50% coverage is enough","Vaccines work by antibiotics","Only the elderly need measles immunity"],
 "Measles R0 is high (~12–18); coverage needs to be around 95%. Herd immunity is a social contract, not a free ride.",
 ["70% is not enough for measles.","R0 is high.","Vaccines are not antibiotics.","Infants and the immunocompromised depend on herd immunity."],
 "One measles case in an ED is an afternoon of contact tracing.","A conscientious objection cluster in a postcode.","Measles control needs very high vaccine coverage.","Australian Immunisation Handbook / WHO", age_label="12-month-old"),

I("public_health","ph-mmr-autism","Public Health","Vaccination","easy","F",28,
 "the GP clinic","refusing MMR for her child because of a claimed autism link",
 "first child, otherwise well",
 "You have time for a proper conversation.",
 "The Wakefield paper was fraudulent and retracted; large studies show no link.",
 "What is the most appropriate evidence-based statement?",
 "High-quality evidence shows no causal link between MMR and autism; delaying MMR leaves the child at risk of measles, mumps, and rubella",
 ["The Wakefield paper remains the gold standard","Split vaccines as proven to reduce autism","Measles is a trivial illness in all children","No conversation is needed — mock her"],
 "The autism–MMR claim is false. Respectful conversation plus firm recommendation works better than ridicule. Offer to vaccinate today.",
 ["Retracted and fraudulent.","Splitting only delays protection.","Measles still kills and causes SSPE.","Ridicule hardens refusal."],
 "Ask what they have read and start there.","A fight in the waiting room.","MMR does not cause autism; recommend on-time vaccination.","Australian Immunisation Handbook / WHO"),

I("public_health","ph-closing-gap","Public Health","Population health","moderate","F",0,
 "the policy briefing","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander life-expectancy gap",
 "you are asked for a high-yield clinical contribution",
 "This is a population item, not a single patient vignette beyond the briefing.",
 "CVD, smoking, rheumatic heart disease, and access are major drivers.",
 "What is a high-yield clinical contribution to Closing the Gap?",
 "Culturally safe, early CVD-risk treatment, smoking cessation, RHD prevention, immunisation, and Aboriginal-led models of care — not a one-off lecture on 'compliance'",
 ["Ignoring the gap because it is 'political'","A deficit lecture without access or cultural safety","Assuming biological inferiority","Ceasing 715 health checks"],
 "The gap is colonial, social, and medical. Clinicians contribute with early prevention, RHD, immunisation, and by not being the barrier.",
 ["Health is political and clinical.","Compliance rhetoric blames patients for system failure.","Racist biology is false.","715 checks are a tool."],
 "Ask what the community wants, then resource it.","A one-hour cultural-awareness slideshow as the whole solution.","Close the Gap with early prevention and culturally safe care.","AHMAC / NACCHO / Closing the Gap", age_label="population-level"),

I("public_health","ph-q-fever","Public Health","Prevention","moderate","M",29,
 "the GP clinic","abattoir worker, Queensland, never vaccinated for Q fever, flu-like illness after a stillborn calf",
 "fever, severe headache, dry cough",
 "Hepatosplenomegaly mild, no rash of measles.",
 "LFTs mixed, thrombocytopenia mild. Q fever serology/PCR pending. He was not immune on pre-vaccination testing years ago and never completed vaccination.",
 "What prevention should his workplace have offered?",
 "Q fever vaccination (after pre-screening) for abattoir, farm, and other high-risk workers",
 ["No occupational vaccine exists","BCG as Q fever prevention","Meningococcal B vaccine as specific for Coxiella","Oseltamivir as Q fever prophylaxis"],
 "Q fever (Coxiella burnetii) is an Australian occupational disease of livestock workers. Vaccination after skin test/serology screening prevents it.",
 ["Q-Vax exists.","BCG is for TB.","Wrong organism.","Oseltamivir is influenza."],
 "Pre-vaccination screening avoids vaccinating the already immune (risk of severe local reaction).","Sending an unvaccinated student to the abattoir.","Vaccinate at-risk workers against Q fever.","Australian Immunisation Handbook"),

I("public_health","ph-ross-river","Public Health","Epidemiology","easy","F",38,
 "the GP clinic","after a camping trip on the Murray, polyarthritis, rash, fatigue",
 "mosquito bites remembered",
 "Polyarthritis, maculopapular rash, no septic joint.",
 "RRV serology IgM positive, other arboviruses considered. No specific antiviral.",
 "What is the most likely diagnosis?",
 "Ross River virus infection",
 ["Rheumatoid arthritis of 5 days' duration with a camping-rash prodrome as more likely","Parvovirus as impossible but RRV impossible too","Gout in 20 joints","Measles with polyarthritis as typical in a vaccinated 38-year-old"],
 "RRV: mosquito-borne, Australia, arthralgia/arthritis, rash, fatigue that can last months. Treatment is supportive. Prevention is mosquito control.",
 ["RA is chronic, not a 5-day camping illness.","Parvovirus is a differential; the stem is classic RRV.","Gout is typically mono/oligo.","Measles is a different illness."],
 "Not every polyarthritis needs methotrexate on day 5.","Doxycycline as a 'cure' for RRV (it is viral).","Recognise Ross River virus clinically.","CDNA / Australian arbovirus guidance"),

I("public_health","ph-melioidosis","Public Health","Epidemiology","difficult","M",54,
 "the Darwin hospital","diabetes, wet season, muddy flood water, fever and a rapidly cavitating pneumonia",
 "septic, travel only within the Top End",
 "Glucose high, no HIV. CXR: upper-lobe cavity.",
 "Blood cultures pending. Burkholderia pseudomallei is the local fear.",
 "What is the most likely diagnosis?",
 "Melioidosis",
 ["Typical pneumococcal pneumonia as more likely in a cavitating wet-season Top End diabetic","TB as the only cavity in the Top End wet season","Q fever as the leading cavitating pneumonia here","Influenza as the cause of a cavity on day 2"],
 "Melioidosis: Top End, wet season, diabetes/alcohol/CKD, pneumonia/sepsis/abscesses. Treat with meropenem/ceftazidime then prolonged eradication (TMP–SMX).",
 ["Strep pneumo rarely cavities this fast in this context.","TB is still possible but the epidemiology screams melioid.","Q fever is more hepatitis/fever.","Influenza does not cavity on day 2."],
 "A diabetic in Darwin in February with sepsis is melioid until cultures return.","Ceftriaxone alone as if it were Melbourne CAP.","Treat Top End wet-season sepsis as melioidosis.","NT guidelines / Therapeutic Guidelines"),

I("public_health","ph-tb-screening","Public Health","Screening principles","moderate","F",31,
 "the TB clinic","born in a high-incidence country, starting TNF-inhibitor for Crohn disease",
 "asymptomatic, CXR normal",
 "No cough. BCG scar present.",
 "Interferon-gamma release assay positive. HIV negative.",
 "What is the most appropriate action before the TNF inhibitor?",
 "Treat latent TB infection first (or at least start LTBI treatment) because TNF inhibitors cause reactivation",
 ["Ignore LTBI because the CXR is normal","Start infliximab today and think about TB if she coughs in 2 years","BCG vaccination now as treatment of LTBI","Isolation in a negative-pressure room indefinitely as an asymptomatic LTBI patient"],
 "IGRA (or TST) before biologics. LTBI treatment (e.g. isoniazid or rifampicin regimens) reduces reactivation. BCG scar does not prevent the need to test.",
 ["CXR does not exclude LTBI.","Reactivation can be disseminated and fatal.","BCG is not LTBI treatment.","She is not infectious if truly latent."],
 "Biologics plus untreated LTBI is a disaster waiting.","A normal CXR as a green light for infliximab.","Treat LTBI before TNF inhibitors.","Australian TB guidelines / TGA"),

I("public_health","ph-bbv-healthcare","Public Health","Prevention","moderate","M",35,
 "occupational health","a surgeon, needlestick from a high-risk source, source HIV-positive, viral load unknown",
 "2 hours ago, deep hollow-bore injury",
 "Surgeon HBV-immune, HCV status negative last year.",
 "HIV PEP should start as soon as possible, ideally within 2 hours, not after 72 hours if it can be started now.",
 "What is the most time-critical prevention?",
 "Start HIV post-exposure prophylaxis immediately after counselling and baseline tests that do not delay the first dose",
 ["Wait for the source viral load next week before any PEP","HCV immunoglobulin as standard","No HBV check because he thinks he was vaccinated in school without titres","Antiretroviral PEP starting at day 10 as equivalent"],
 "HIV PEP is a few-hours decision. Baseline serology should not delay the first dose. HCV has no standard PEP; HBV depends on immunity.",
 ["Do not wait.","No routine HCV PEP.","Confirm titres.","Day 10 is too late for best effect."],
 "PEP packs should be in the ED, not in a locked office off-site at 2 am.","A 24-hour wait for ID 'approval' of PEP.","Start HIV PEP immediately after significant exposure.","ASHM / state PEP guidelines"),

I("public_health","ph-hand-hygiene","Public Health","Prevention","easy","F",0,
 "the ward","a cluster of MRSA in a bay",
 "you are asked for the single most effective daily behaviour to cut transmission",
 "This is a staff-behaviour item.",
 "Gloves are not a substitute for hand hygiene.",
 "What is the highest-yield routine measure?",
 "Alcohol-based hand rub at the 5 Moments, with soap and water for spore-forming organisms (C. difficile) and when hands are visibly soiled",
 ["Gloves instead of hand hygiene","A white coat as protection enough","Antibiotics for all staff","Closing the hospital permanently"],
 "Hand hygiene is the core of infection control. ABHR for most moments; soap for C. difficile spores.",
 ["Gloves get contaminated.","Coats are not a strategy.","Staff antibiotics are not the answer.","Disproportionate."],
 "The 5 Moments are not optional.","Gel then immediately answering a contaminated phone then a cannula.","Use the 5 Moments of hand hygiene.","Hand Hygiene Australia / WHO", age_label="staff-level"),

I("public_health","ph-isolation","Public Health","Prevention","moderate","M",4,
 "the emergency department","measles, unvaccinated, fever and rash day 3",
 "coughing in a shared waiting room for 40 minutes",
 "Koplik spots, conjunctivitis, rash from the face down.",
 "Airborne precautions. Contact tracing for the waiting room.",
 "What is the correct isolation?",
 "Immediate airborne isolation (negative pressure if available), and contact-trace exposed patients and staff for immunoglobulin/vaccine as indicated",
 ["Standard precautions only","Droplet only without airborne as sufficient for measles","No tracing of a 40-minute shared waiting room","Antibiotics as the isolation method"],
 "Measles is airborne and lingers. Isolate, notify, and give PEP (vaccine within 72 h or immunoglobulin as indicated) to susceptibles.",
 ["Standard is not enough.","Airborne, not only droplet.","Waiting rooms are exposure sites.","Antibiotics do not isolate."],
 "A measles case is a public-health incident, not 'just a viral rash'.","Leaving them in the waiting room for a second set of observations.","Airborne isolation and PEP for measles exposures.","CDNA measles guidelines", age_label="4-year-old"),

I("public_health","ph-rct","Public Health","Biostatistics","easy","F",60,
 "the journal club","a new drug, investigators allocated the sickest patients to the old drug by choice",
 "they report the new drug looks better",
 "No randomisation, no concealment.",
 "Confounding by indication is likely.",
 "What study design would best support a causal treatment claim?",
 "A randomised controlled trial with allocation concealment",
 ["An uncontrolled case series of survivors","A social-media poll","Expert opinion only","A before–after study in a single ward with no control"],
 "Randomisation, properly concealed, balances known and unknown confounders. Observational comparisons of who got which drug are biased.",
 ["Case series cannot prove superiority.","Polls are not evidence.","Opinion is the bottom of the pyramid.","Before–after is confounded by time."],
 "If they could choose who got the drug, they did.","A registry comparison sold as an RCT.","RCTs with concealment best support causal treatment claims.","Standard EBM"),

I("public_health","ph-confounding","Public Health","Biostatistics","moderate","M",70,
 "the journal club","coffee drinkers have lower mortality in a cohort, but they also smoke less, exercise more, and are richer in this dataset",
 "authors claim coffee causes immortality",
 "No randomisation. Some adjustment was done for age only.",
 "Residual confounding is likely.",
 "What is the main threat to inference?",
 "Confounding: other variables associated with both coffee and death may explain the association",
 ["Perfect causation proven by any cohort","Recall bias as the only possible issue in a prospective cohort with measured coffee","Placebo effect on all-cause mortality in an observational coffee study as the main threat","Loss of allocation concealment in a non-randomised study as a real RCT problem"],
 "Confounding is the ghost in observational epidemiology. Adjustment helps but cannot guarantee. RCTs of coffee are rare; be humble.",
 ["Cohorts show association.","Recall is less of an issue if data were collected prospectively.","No placebo was given.","There was no allocation to conceal."],
 "Ask 'who drinks coffee in this population?'.","A headline that coffee prevents death, full stop.","Confounding can explain observational associations.","Standard epidemiology"),

I("public_health","ph-itt","Public Health","Biostatistics","moderate","F",64,
 "the journal club","an RCT of a drug, 30% of the treatment arm stopped the drug; authors analyse only those who continued ('completers') and find a huge benefit",
 "the question is about analysis",
 "Randomisation is broken by excluding non-adherers.",
 "Intention-to-treat keeps participants in the arm they were randomised to.",
 "What is the preferred primary analysis for a pragmatic effectiveness question?",
 "Intention-to-treat",
 ["As-treated analysis as the least biased primary analysis when dropouts are informative","Excluding all who did poorly in the treatment arm","Per-protocol as always unbiased","Analysing only survivors"],
 "ITT answers 'what happens if we offer this treatment?' and preserves randomisation. Per-protocol answers a different, more biased question when adherence is related to prognosis.",
 ["As-treated reintroduces confounding.","That is fraudulent cherry-picking.","Per-protocol is not unbiased.","Survivor analysis is biased."],
 "If they dropped out because of side-effects, that is part of the treatment's story.","A completer analysis sold as the primary ITT.","Use intention-to-treat as the primary RCT analysis.","CONSORT / standard EBM"),

I("public_health","ph-screening-harm","Public Health","Screening principles","moderate","M",55,
 "the GP clinic","asks about a new prostate-screening kit advertised online with no mortality benefit in trials and high incontinence/erectile-dysfunction rates after treatment of screen-detected disease",
 "he is average risk",
 "You discuss overdiagnosis.",
 "He thinks screening can only help.",
 "What harm of screening must you mention?",
 "Overdiagnosis and overtreatment: detecting disease that would not have caused harm, then exposing people to treatment complications",
 ["Screening never harms","False negatives as the only possible harm","Cost to the manufacturer as the main ethical harm","Radiation of a blood test"],
 "Screening harms: false positives, overdiagnosis, overtreatment, anxiety, cost, and (for some tests) radiation/invasive follow-up. Benefits must outweigh these.",
 ["All screening has a harm profile.","False negatives matter too, but overdiagnosis is the neglected harm.","Not the main issue.","PSA is a blood test."],
 "The best test finds disease that matters.","A billboard that says 'screening saves lives' with no numbers.","Discuss overdiagnosis as a screening harm.","Wilson and Jungner / RACGP red book"),

I("public_health","ph-notifiable-measles","Public Health","Epidemiology","easy","F",8,
 "the emergency department","suspected measles in an unvaccinated child just back from overseas",
 "fever, cough, conjunctivitis, morbilliform rash",
 "Koplik spots. Airborne isolation started.",
 "IgM/PCR pending. You are the treating doctor.",
 "What legal public-health step is required?",
 "Urgent notification to the public-health unit (by phone, not waiting for serology) because measles is immediately notifiable",
 ["Wait 2 weeks for IgG avidity before telling anyone","A letter in a month","Notification only if there is a death","Telling the school yourself as a substitute for the PH unit"],
 "Measles is urgent: phone the PH unit the same hour. They run contact tracing. You still isolate and treat supportively.",
 ["Do not wait for perfect serology.","Too slow.","One case is enough.","The PH unit coordinates schools."],
 "Suspect, isolate, notify — in that order, immediately.","A discharge home from the waiting room with a 'viral rash'.","Phone-notify suspected measles immediately.","CDNA / state PH", age_label="8-year-old"),

I("public_health","ph-smoking-prevalence","Public Health","Population health","easy","M",0,
 "the policy seminar","you are asked which single intervention has the largest population effect on tobacco use",
 "clinical NRT is important but not the whole story",
 "This is a population item.",
 "Price, smoke-free laws, advertising bans, and quit support all contribute.",
 "What population measure has the strongest evidence for reducing smoking prevalence?",
 "A combination of tax/price increases, smoke-free legislation, advertising bans, and accessible cessation support (including Aboriginal-led programs)",
 ["School posters alone as sufficient","A one-off GP lecture without policy","Subsidising cigarettes for equity without other measures","Banning cessation medicines"],
 "Tobacco control is a package. Tax is among the most effective population levers; clinical cessation still matters for the individual in front of you.",
 ["Education alone underperforms.","Policy plus clinical care.","Price cuts increase harm.","Medicines help quitters."],
 "The patient in front of you still deserves NRT even while you support tax.","A poster in the tea room as the hospital's tobacco policy.","Use tax, law, and cessation support together against tobacco.","WHO FCTC / Australian tobacco strategy", age_label="population-level"),

I("public_health","ph-rhd-prevention","Public Health","Prevention","moderate","F",12,
 "the remote clinic","Aboriginal girl, first episode of acute rheumatic fever, now recovered",
 "you are planning secondary prevention",
 "Carditis was mild. Echo follow-up planned.",
 "GAS carriage and overcrowding are community issues.",
 "What is the most important individual prevention of recurrent ARF/RHD?",
 "Regular intramuscular benzathine penicillin secondary prophylaxis for the guideline duration, plus community GAS control",
 ["One-off oral amoxicillin as equivalent lifelong prevention","No follow-up echo","Aspirin as secondary prophylaxis of ARF","Influenza vaccine as a substitute for penicillin prophylaxis"],
 "RHD is a Closing-the-Gap disease. Secondary penicillin prevents recurrences. Primary prevention is treating Strep A sore throats/impetigo in high-risk settings.",
 ["Duration is years, not one dose.","Echo surveillance is required.","Aspirin is for the acute arthritis/carditis symptom, not long-term prevention.","Wrong prevention."],
 "Put the next injection date in the recall system before they leave.","A missed penicillin dose in a high-risk community.","Use benzathine penicillin secondary prophylaxis after ARF.","RHDAustralia guideline", age_label="12-year-old"),

I("public_health","ph-hiv-stigma","Public Health","Population health","easy","M",30,
 "the GP clinic","new HIV diagnosis, undetectable on treatment, asks if he can have sex with his partner without transmitting",
 "partner is negative, considering PrEP",
 "You know U=U (undetectable = untransmittable) for sexual transmission when VL is stably suppressed.",
 "Condoms still prevent other STIs. Partner notification is needed.",
 "What is the most accurate public-health statement?",
 "Sustained undetectable viral load means sexual HIV transmission risk is effectively zero (U=U); still offer partner PrEP/condoms for other STIs and complete public-health notification",
 ["He must never have sex again","U=U is false in all Australian guidance","No partner notification is required for HIV","Breastfeeding transmission risk is also zero in all settings without specialist advice"],
 "U=U is evidence-based for sexual transmission. HIV remains notifiable. Infant feeding advice is specialist and setting-dependent.",
 ["Criminalising language is harmful and inaccurate.","U=U is accepted.","Notification is required.","Vertical/breastfeeding advice is separate."],
 "Treat the virus and the stigma.","A 1980s lecture in a 2026 clinic.","U=U for sexual transmission when viral load is undetectable.","ASHM / WHO"),
]
